{"body": "Conjecture: the function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule says \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction,\" gives the same answer for two replies that the rule requires it to tell apart. The first reply is: ```python\n{\"a\": 1}\n```\n{\"b\": 2}. The second reply is: {\"b\": 2}. For the first reply, the rule requires the object {\"a\": 1}, because it is the last object inside a code fence (the ```python fence), and any fence holds one, so the later top-level object {\"b\": 2} is ignored. For the second reply, the rule requires {\"b\": 2}, because there is no fence and it is the last top-level object. The function, however, returns {\"b\": 2} for both: its fence regex only matches fences tagged json or JSON or with no tag, so the ```python fence is not collected, and the function falls through to the top-level pool, where the last object is {\"b\": 2}. The observation that would show this conjecture false is recover_json_object returning {\"a\": 1} for the first reply (i.e., recognizing the ```python fence as a code fence) or returning anything other than {\"b\": 2} for the second reply."}